# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local environment — DO NOT commit this file.
# After switching to Postgres, you need a real DATABASE_URL.
# Easiest path: create a free Neon DB at https://neon.tech
# and paste the connection string below.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

DATABASE_URL="file:./dev.db"

JWT_SECRET="meridian-hr-dev-secret-change-in-production-9f8a7b6c5d4e3f2a1b0c9d8e7f6a5b4c"

# Step 1 — Push your code to GitHub (5 min)
# Run these in your Terminal:


# cd /Users/divinehindu/Downloads/Meridian_HR_Portal
# git init
# git add .
# git commit -m "Initial commit: Meridian HR Portal"
# Now go to https://github.com/new in your browser:

# Repository name: meridian-hr
# Privacy: Private (recommended)
# Do not check "Add a README" / "Add .gitignore" — your project already has them
# Click Create repository
# GitHub will show a screen with a section called "…or push an existing repository from the command line". Copy those three lines and run them. They look like this (replace YOUR_USERNAME):


# git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/meridian-hr.git
# git branch -M main
# git push -u origin main
# When it finishes, refresh your repo page — you should see all your files.

# Step 2 — Create a free Neon Postgres database (3 min)
# Open https://console.neon.tech → click Sign in with GitHub → authorize.
# After login you'll see a "Create your first project" page (or click New Project if you've signed in before).
# Fill in:
# Project name: meridian-hr
# Postgres version: 16 (default)
# Region: pick the one closest to you (e.g. AWS US East (Ohio))
# Click Create Project.
# Neon shows you a Connection string at the top of the dashboard. It looks like:

# postgresql://meridian-hr_owner:abc123XYZ@ep-cool-snow-12345.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require
# Click the copy icon next to it. Keep this string handy — you'll paste it three times.
# Step 3 — Update your local .env (2 min)
# Open .env in your editor. Replace the contents with:


# DATABASE_URL="<paste your Neon connection string here>"
# JWT_SECRET="<paste output of openssl rand -hex 32 here>"
# To generate a JWT_SECRET, run this in Terminal:


# openssl rand -hex 32
# It prints something like a3f7e2c9b1d4.... Paste that as JWT_SECRET.

# Your .env should now look like (example values):


# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://meridian-hr_owner:abc123XYZ@ep-cool-snow-12345.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require"
# JWT_SECRET="a3f7e2c9b1d4e8f0a1b2c3d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4"
# Save the file.

# Step 4 — Create tables in Neon and load the 116 employees (1 min)
# Back in Terminal:


# cd /Users/divinehindu/Downloads/Meridian_HR_Portal
# npm run db:deploy
# This:

# Runs prisma db push → creates 15 tables in Neon
# Runs tsx prisma/seed.ts → inserts 116 employees, 358 goals, 714 training enrollments, etc.
# You'll see green checkmarks for each: ✓ Seeded 116 employees, etc. Takes about 30 seconds.

# Step 5 — Verify it works locally before deploying (1 min)

# npm run dev
# Open http://localhost:5173/ in your browser and log in with:

# Email: hr.admin@meridian.co
# Password: demopassword
# Make sure the dashboard loads with 116 employees. If it does, your local app is now talking to Neon successfully. Press Ctrl+C to stop the dev server.

# Step 6 — Deploy to Vercel (3 min)
# Go to https://vercel.com/new → sign in with GitHub if you haven't.

# You'll see a list of your repos. Click Import next to meridian-hr.

# On the "Configure Project" screen:

# Framework Preset: Next.js (auto-detected — leave as is)
# Root Directory: ./ (default)
# Build Command, Install Command, Output Directory: all default (Vercel reads vercel.json)
# Expand Environment Variables. Add two variables:

# Name	Value
# DATABASE_URL	the same Neon connection string from Step 2
# JWT_SECRET	the same random string from Step 3
# Click Add after each one.

# Click Deploy.

# Vercel now:

# Clones your repo from GitHub
# Runs npm install
# Runs prisma generate && next build
# Deploys to its edge network
# This takes ~2 minutes. You'll see logs scrolling. When it finishes, a confetti animation appears with your URL — something like https://meridian-hr-abc123.vercel.app.

# Step 7 — Test the live URL (1 min)
# Click the Vercel URL. You should see the login page. Sign in:

# Email: hr.admin@meridian.co
# Password: demopassword
# Verify:

# ✅ Dashboard loads with 116 employees
# ✅ Click Employees → all 116 are listed
# ✅ Click any employee → profile shows payslip, goals, training
# ✅ Click Download Excel → downloads Meridian_HR_Dashboard_<date>.xlsx
# ✅ Click Import Data → upload page renders
# ✅ Resize browser to mobile width → hamburger menu appears
# If all of that works, you're live. Share the URL.

# When you make changes later
# Edit code → commit → push:


# git add .
# git commit -m "your message"
# git push
# Vercel auto-detects the push, rebuilds, and replaces the live site in ~90 seconds. Your Neon database is untouched between deploys — only your data lives there.

# If you change prisma/schema.prisma (add a column, etc.), run once before pushing:


# npm run db:push
# This pushes the schema change to Neon. Then git push deploys the code that uses it.

# Common errors and fixes
# Symptom	Fix
# npm run db:deploy says "Can't reach database"	Your DATABASE_URL in .env is wrong — re-copy from Neon dashboard, keep the ?sslmode=require at the end
# Vercel build fails at prisma generate	The DATABASE_URL env var on Vercel is missing or malformed. Project Settings → Environment Variables → re-paste
# Live site shows login but rejects valid creds	The Neon DB doesn't have seeded users. Run npm run db:seed locally (your .env points to Neon, so it seeds production)
# First request to live site takes 2-3 seconds	Neon free tier sleeps after ~5 min idle and wakes on demand. Normal. Subsequent requests are instant
# Forgot Neon password	Neon dashboard → Project → Branches → main → Reset password — gives you a fresh connection string
# Optional polish later
# Custom domain — Vercel → Project → Settings → Domains → add your domain → follow DNS instructions. Free, just need to own a domain (~$10/yr at any registrar).
# Disable the demo-credentials box on the login page — open app/login/page.tsx and delete the "Demo accounts" block at the bottom of the form.
# Add real HR users — edit prisma/seed.ts and re-run npm run db:seed, or build a /users admin page later.
# Just tell me when you've finished step 7 (or hit any error) and I'll help with the next move.



